# The BROWSER's half of this repo's configuration (apps/web). Canonical list.
#
# Copy to `apps/web/.env.local` (git-ignored) and edit, or leave it alone: every value
# below IS the local default, so a verbatim copy changes nothing about how the app
# behaves on a dev machine. Next loads `.env*` from the PACKAGE directory — this one —
# and never from the repo root, so the root `.env` the API reads has no effect here.
#
# `next build` INLINES every `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` value into the JavaScript bundle. Three
# consequences, and all three are why this file exists rather than a section of the
# root `.env.example`:
#
#   1. A key that is misspelled, renamed or absent does not throw. It compiles to the
#      empty string, ships, and fails as a broken screen in front of a client.
#      `scripts/check_web_env_parity.py` is the check that makes that a failed BUILD
#      instead: every key read by `apps/web` is declared here, every key declared here
#      is read, and neither direction is allowed to drift.
#   2. Changing one needs a REBUILD, not a restart. There is no runtime override.
#   3. NOTHING HERE IS PRIVATE. Every value is served to every visitor as plain text in
#      the bundle. A secret does not become one by living in this file, which is why
#      the guardrail refuses a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` name shaped like a credential unless
#      `PUBLIC_BY_DESIGN` in that script records why that particular value is public.
#      Server-side secrets stay in the root `.env.example` + `Settings`.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE. The root `.env.example` is checked BOTH WAYS against
# `calevate_shared.config.Settings` (scripts/check_env_parity.py), and none of these
# may be Settings fields: the API neither reads them nor should be able to (D-49 set
# that precedent moving PostHog out of Settings). Declaring them there therefore forced
# them to be COMMENTS, which is a declaration no machine can check — the gap this file
# closes. Living beside `apps/web/.env.local`, the file Next actually loads, also means
# a developer copies the template that belongs to the app they are running.

# --- what credential the browser presents ------------------------------------
# `clerk` | `dev` | empty. Empty = `dev` locally and `clerk` in a production build;
# an explicit `dev` in a production build FAILS THE BUILD, because the dev credential
# is `dev:<realm>:<user-id>` and the API accepts it whenever APP_ENV=local and the
# realm has no Clerk secret — shipping it would let anyone sign in as anyone.
# `src/lib/auth/mode.ts` carries the full argument and both guards.
NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_MODE=

# The two Clerk applications' publishable keys (`pk_test_...` / `pk_live_...`), one per
# realm — TRD §11 and D-37 keep admin and client in SEPARATE applications with no shared
# session logic. They must name the SAME applications as CLERK_CLIENT_PUBLISHABLE_KEY /
# CLERK_ADMIN_PUBLISHABLE_KEY in the root `.env.example`: the API derives each realm's
# JWKS host from its copy (`apps/api/core/auth.py::jwks_url`), so a mismatch is a browser
# minting tokens no verifier will accept.
#
# Deliberately NOT `NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`, which is the realm-blind name
# @clerk/nextjs picks up on its own — a default that would let a provider mount the wrong
# realm's application silently. Empty = that realm renders "sign-in is not configured"
# and refuses every request; it never falls back to a dev token.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_CLIENT_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_ADMIN_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# --- where the browser sends its requests ------------------------------------
# NOT empty on purpose. `src/lib/api/client.ts` reads this as
# `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL ?? "http://localhost:8000"`, and `??` falls back
# on undefined — NOT on the empty string a bare `KEY=` line produces. An empty value
# here would therefore make every request relative and 404 against the Next server,
# which is exactly the silent-empty-string failure this file is about. The guardrail
# refuses an empty declaration whose reader supplies a non-empty default.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# --- local identities (NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_MODE=dev only) ------------------------
# Who the `dev:<realm>:<id>` token claims to be. Same non-empty rule as above: the
# readers default to these exact values, so an empty line would mint `dev:client:` and
# the API would refuse it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_USER=user_local
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_ADMIN=admin_local
# The tenant slug the landing page links to for local work. Empty = no such link is
# rendered, which is the correct state in every deployed build.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_ORG_SLUG=

# --- the public signup door (D-34 motion 2) -----------------------------------
# The BROWSER's copy of SELF_SERVE_SIGNUP_ENABLED. `"true"` opens the door in the UI;
# anything else closes it. The server is still authoritative — `src/lib/api/signup.ts`
# keeps its runtime refusals wired precisely because an inlined value can be stale — so
# this only decides what the screen offers, never what the API allows.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SELF_SERVE_SIGNUP_ENABLED=false
# Who to talk to when the online door is shut. Empty renders the sentence without a
# link rather than inventing an address that would bounce.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNUP_CONTACT_EMAIL=
